<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Stairs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Stairs was a Canadian-born British army officer and explorer. After serving on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a>'s Emin Pasha Relief Expedition, he led an expedition for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> in 1891–1892, racing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a>. At the Yeke capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunkeya">Bunkeya</a> the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/msiri">Msiri</a> was shot dead in December 1891, securing Katanga for the Free State. Stairs died of malaria at Chinde on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> in 1892.</p></div>
